      "id": "ielts_read_10",
      "difficulty": "중",
      "category": "IELTS Reading (Band 6.0~6.5)",
      "title": "Ocean Acidification and Coral Bleaching Phenomena",
      "passage": "Often hailed as the 'rainforests of the marine kingdom', tropical coral reefs support\nover twenty-five percent of all marine species despite occupying under one percent of the ocean floor.\nThese complex calcium-carbonate bio-structures provide crucial coastal storm buffers\nand sustain the livelihoods and food security of half a billion people globally.\nTragically, global anthropogenic climate destabilisation is placing these fragile ecosystems in jeopardy.\nThe world's oceans have absorbed approximately thirty percent of excess carbon dioxide generated by humans,\ntriggering widespread ocean acidification that severely hampers calcifying coral polyps from building skeletons.\nSimultaneously, prolonged marine heatwaves induce catastrophic thermal stress on coral colonies.\nIn response to elevated water temperatures, corals expel their symbiotic photosynthetic microalgae,\nknown scientifically as zooxanthellae, which inhabit their tissues and furnish up to ninety percent of their nutrients.\nDeprived of these vibrant algae, the coral skeletons turn ghostly white in a process known as 'bleaching'.\nWhile bleached corals are not dead immediately, prolonged nutrient starvation leaves them highly vulnerable to disease.\nMarine conservation biologists emphasise that without urgent worldwide decarbonisation efforts,\nalong with local interventions like cultivating thermally resilient 'super-corals' in gene nurseries,\nmost shallow-water tropical reefs could face irreversible ecological extinction by the century's conclusion.\nProtecting these vibrant marine sanctuaries is an urgent imperative for global ecological survival.",
