      "difficulty": "중",
      "category": "IELTS Reading (Band 6.0~6.5)",
      "title": "The Evolution and Dual Impact of Ecotourism",
      "passage": "Conceived in the late twentieth century as an ethical alternative to exploitative mass travel,\necotourism promised to harmonise environmental conservation with socio-economic empowerment.\nThe core ethos prioritises low-impact travel to fragile, pristine natural ecosystems,\nchannelling tourism revenues directly into wildlife protection and indigenous community development.\nIn classic success stories, such as mountain gorilla conservation across Rwanda and Uganda,\nlucrative visitor permit fees have turned former wildlife poachers into passionate park rangers.\nLocal communities receive sustained financing for potable water infrastructure, schools, and health clinics,\nincentivising them to safeguard endangered species rather than encroach upon forest reserves.\nHowever, the commercial boom of ecotourism has inevitably birthed disquieting unintended paradoxes.\nThe relentless surge of tourists into previously secluded biodiversity hotspots often requires\nthe construction of paved access roads, safari airstrips, and luxury wilderness lodges.\nHeavy vehicular traffic fragments habitats, contaminates local watersheds with sewage run-off,\nand alters the foraging behaviours of territorial apex predators.\nFurthermore, critics highlight 'greenwashing', where unscrupulous operators brand conventional holidays\nas 'eco-friendly' without instituting genuine environmental protocols or benefiting native residents.\nGenuine ecotourism requires uncompromising regulatory standards and robust community governance.",
