        "title": "Microplastics in Freshwater Ecosystems and Trophic Transfer",
        "passage": "While global scientific scrutiny has concentrated extensively on marine plastic debris,\nenvironmental toxicologists are sounding alarms regarding microplastic contamination in freshwater river basins.\nMicroplastics—defined as synthetic polymer particles measuring less than five millimetres in diameter—\noriginate from the degradation of commercial packaging, tyre abrasion, and synthetic textile fibres shedding in laundry.\nInland waterways and lakes serve as primary conduits transporting these persistent synthetic pollutants from land to sea,\nyet substantial quantities settle permanently into riverbeds or remain suspended in the water column.\nAquatic organisms at the base of the food web, including filter-feeding zooplankton, benthic larvae, and small fish,\nreadily mistake these buoyant colourful plastic particles for nutrient-rich food sources.\nIngestion induces severe internal abrasions, pseudo-satiety leading to starvation, and endocrine disruptions.\nCrucially, microplastics possess hydrophobic surfaces that readily absorb toxic industrial chemical contaminants,\nsuch as polychlorinated biphenyls (PCBs) and heavy metals, from ambient waters at alarmingly high concentrations.\nAs primary consumers are predated by apex predators, these toxic particles bioaccumulate across trophic levels,\neventually reaching commercial fish species harvested for widespread human dietary consumption.\nMitigating freshwater plastic contamination demands comprehensive interventions: from wastewater filtration retrofits\nto redesigning textiles and enforcing strict extended producer responsibility frameworks across industrial supply chains.",
