        "category": "IELTS Reading (Band 7.0~8.0)",
        "title": "Neuroplasticity, Cognitive Reserve, and Healthy Brain Ageing",
        "passage": "For over a century, the classical dogma in neurobiology asserted that the adult human brain\nwas an immutable, hard-wired organ incapable of generating novel neurons or restructuring circuits.\nIt was widely assumed that post-adolescent cerebral development was purely a gradual process\nof inexorable neurodegenerative decline, where lost cortical capacity could never be reclaimed.\nGroundbreaking neuroimaging breakthroughs in contemporary neuroscience have decisively shattered\nthis fatalistic orthodoxy, establishing the lifelong persistence of neuroplasticity—the extraordinary\ncapacity of neural networks to continually reorganise, form synaptic connections, and even undergo neurogenesis.\nCrucially, the degree to which an ageing brain preserves robust executive functioning is underpinned\nby the conceptual paradigm of 'cognitive reserve'. Coined to elucidate a paradoxical clinical anomaly,\ncognitive reserve explains why certain elderly individuals demonstrate minimal cognitive impairment\nin life despite autopsy post-mortems revealing advanced pathological hallmarks of Alzheimer's disease.\nRather than possessing structural immunity to neuropathological lesions, these resilient brains exhibit\nsuperior functional redundancy and cognitive flexibility: when primary computational pathways deteriorate,\nthe brain fluidly recruits alternative neural circuits across bilateral cerebral hemispheres to execute tasks.\nDecades of empirical epidemiological tracking indicate that cognitive reserve is not biologically predetermined;\nrather, it is actively accumulated across a lifespan through demanding intellectual and social pursuits.\nSustained mastery of complex skills—such as acquiring multiple languages, learning polyphonic musical instruments,\nor navigating cognitively taxing professional vocations—stimulates continuous dendritic branching.\nConcurrently, regular aerobic physical exercise triggers the upregulation of Brain-Derived Neurotrophic Factor (BDNF),\na master biochemical catalyst that promotes synaptic plasticity and preserves hippocampal volume.\nFurthermore, rich interpersonal social engagement provides potent cognitive buffering against loneliness,\na chronic psychological stressor known to provoke neurotoxic cortisol cascades and accelerate cortical atrophy.\nThe revelation that brain architecture remains profoundly adaptable into senescence empowers us:\nageing is not a passive surrender to inevitable decay, but an active, lifelong creative project of cognitive enrichment.",
