        "id": "ielts_read_16",
        "difficulty": "상",
        "category": "IELTS Reading (Band 7.0~8.0)",
        "title": "Epigenetics and Environmental Influences on Gene Expression",
        "passage": "Throughout the mid-to-late twentieth century, biological science was overwhelmingly dominated\nby genetic determinism—the reductionist conviction that an organism's physical traits, disease susceptibilities,\nand behavioural trajectories were rigidly, immutably hard-coded into the static four-letter nucleotide sequence of DNA.\nUnder this rigid paradigm, our inherited genome was cast as an unalterable biological fate.\nHowever, the burgeoning frontier of epigenetics has utterly dismantled this fatalistic view,\nrevealing that while the underlying primary DNA sequence remains largely unchanged throughout life,\nthe operational manifestation and activity of those genes is profoundly fluid and responsive to external stimuli.\nEpigenetics—literally meaning 'above' or 'on top of' genetics—investigates the complex molecular mechanisms\nthat control gene expression without altering the underlying genetic text.\nChief among these regulatory mechanisms is DNA methylation, where small chemical carbon-hydrogen tags\nknown as methyl groups attach directly to specific cytosine bases, functioning as microscopic molecular switches\nthat typically silence or dial down the transcriptional expression of adjacent genes.\nAdditionally, histone modification alters the physical packaging of DNA around structural proteins,\ndetermining whether genetic segments are coiled so tightly that cellular machinery cannot access them,\nor relaxed into open configurations readily accessible for transcription into messenger RNA.\nAstoundingly, empirical studies reveal that these biochemical chromatin switches are dynamically tuned\nby a diverse array of environmental exposures, including chronic psychological distress, nutritional quality,\naerobic exercise, environmental toxicant exposure, and circadian disruptions.\nEven more revolutionary is the contentious discovery of transgenerational epigenetic inheritance:\npioneering animal models and human historical epidemiological data demonstrate that severe nutritional deprivation\nor severe psychological trauma experienced by parents can alter the epigenetic marks in their germline cells,\ntransmitting elevated risks of metabolic disorders and anxiety across subsequent generations without altering DNA.\nEpigenetics dissolves the historic philosophical dichotomy between nature and nurture:\nour genes represent not an unyielding dictator, but an exquisite, interactive musical score that our life choices dynamically play.",
        "lineCount": 24,
        "question": "What is the defining discovery of the field of epigenetics regarding human genetics?",
